weinberg-angle-and-field-mixing-2b860847.conceptual_definition:
The Weinberg angle, also known as the weak mixing angle, quantifies the mixing between the neutral gauge bosons of the electroweak force. It specifically describes how the observed, physical photon (
A_\mu
) and Z boson (
Z_\mu
) fields arise as linear combinations of the fundamental, pre-symmetry-breaking gauge fields: the neutral weak isospin field (
W^3_\mu
) and the hypercharge field (
B_\mu
). This mixing is a direct consequence of the spontaneous electroweak symmetry breaking.
